## Summary

Deployment logs and deletion should require access to the service or server that owns the deployment, including when ownership is reachable only through a backup, preview, volume backup, or schedule.

Dokploy's deployment handlers authorize rows only when `applicationId` or `composeId` is set directly, plus one special case for schedules with `serverId`.

Deployments created for preview builds, database backups, volume backups, server setup, and application/Compose schedules do not store those direct IDs. Their owner is reachable through `previewDeploymentId`, `backupId`, `volumeBackupId`, `serverId`, or the schedule's application/Compose relation. `deployment.readLogs` and `deployment.removeDeployment` never resolve those relationships, so any authenticated caller with a deployment ID reaches the log reader or deletion sink without an authorization check. For a direct server deployment, `readLogs` then trusts `deployment.serverId` and reads the foreign log over SSH.

This is a residual variant of the deployment endpoints described in `GHSA-f8wj-5c4w-frhg` / `CVE-2026-43917`.

## Details

The same incomplete ownership switch is duplicated in `killProcess`, `removeDeployment`, and `readLogs`. Its only server check applies to `deployment.schedule?.serverId`. A direct `deployment.serverId` does not enter that branch.

